::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What does MLA.GEO.RP.4 require students to interpret and identify?
{
~A. Only segment and angle constructions#Constructions belong to a different Geometry standard.
=B. Conditional statements, converse, inverse, and contrapositive statements#Correct. The standard focuses on conditional logic and related statement forms.
~C. Only coordinate distance formulas#Coordinate distance is not the focus of this logic standard.
~D. Only numerical equation solving#This lesson focuses on logical statements, not equation solving.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
In the conditional statement If a figure is a square, then it has four sides, what is the hypothesis?
{
~A. It has four sides#This is the conclusion because it follows then.
~B. A figure is not a square#This negates the hypothesis.
~C. It does not have four sides#This negates the conclusion.
=D. A figure is a square#Correct. The hypothesis is the condition that follows if.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
In the conditional statement If a figure is a rectangle, then it has four right angles, what is the conclusion?
{
=A. It has four right angles#Correct. The conclusion is the result that follows then.
~B. A figure is a rectangle#This is the hypothesis because it follows if.
~C. A figure is not a rectangle#This is the negation of the hypothesis.
~D. It does not have four right angles#This is the negation of the conclusion.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which form represents a conditional statement?
{
~A. If q, then p#This is the converse form when starting from If p, then q.
~B. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse form when starting from If p, then q.
=C. If p, then q#Correct. This is the standard symbolic form of a conditional statement.
~D. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive form when starting from If p, then q.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the converse of If two angles are vertical angles, then they are congruent?
{
~A. If two angles are not vertical angles, then they are not congruent#This is the inverse, not the converse.
=B. If two angles are congruent, then they are vertical angles#Correct. The converse switches the hypothesis and conclusion.
~C. If two angles are not congruent, then they are not vertical angles#This is the contrapositive, not the converse.
~D. If two angles are vertical angles, then they are not congruent#This changes the conclusion and is not the converse.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the inverse of If a figure is a triangle, then it has three sides?
{
=A. If a figure is not a triangle, then it does not have three sides#Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
~B. If a figure has three sides, then it is a triangle#This is the converse, not the inverse.
~C. If a figure does not have three sides, then it is not a triangle#This is the contrapositive, not the inverse.
~D. If a figure is a triangle, then it does not have three sides#This negates only the conclusion.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the contrapositive of If a figure is a square, then it is a rectangle?
{
~A. If a figure is a rectangle, then it is a square#This is the converse, not the contrapositive.
~B. If a figure is not a square, then it is not a rectangle#This is the inverse, not the contrapositive.
~C. If a figure is a square, then it is not a rectangle#This negates only the conclusion.
=D. If a figure is not a rectangle, then it is not a square#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which statement is the converse of If p, then q?
{
~A. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse.
~B. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive.
=C. If q, then p#Correct. The converse switches p and q.
~D. If p, then not q#This is not the converse.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which statement is the inverse of If p, then q?
{
=A. If not p, then not q#Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
~B. If q, then p#This is the converse.
~C. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive.
~D. If q, then not p#This is not the inverse form.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which statement is the contrapositive of If p, then q?
{
~A. If q, then p#This is the converse.
~B. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse.
~C. If p, then q#This is the original conditional.
=D. If not q, then not p#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What does all mean in the statement All squares have four sides?
{
~A. Only one square has four sides#All refers to every square, not one example.
=B. Every square has four sides#Correct. All means the statement applies to every object in the group.
~C. No square has four sides#This is the opposite of the statement.
~D. Some squares may not have four sides#All does not allow exceptions within the group.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which if-then statement matches All rectangles have four right angles?
{
~A. If a figure has four right angles, then it is a rectangle#This is the converse of the matching conditional.
~B. If a figure is not a rectangle, then it does not have four right angles#This is the inverse.
=C. If a figure is a rectangle, then it has four right angles#Correct. All rectangles means every rectangle has the stated property.
~D. If a figure does not have four right angles, then it is not a rectangle#This is the contrapositive.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What does not do in a related conditional statement?
{
~A. It switches the order of p and q#Switching order creates the converse.
~B. It turns every statement into a biconditional#Not does not create an if and only if statement.
~C. It removes the hypothesis#The hypothesis remains a statement part even when negated.
=D. It negates a condition or conclusion#Correct. Not denies or reverses the meaning of a statement part.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What does if and only if mean?
{
=A. Both the conditional and converse directions are included#Correct. A biconditional combines both directions.
~B. Only the conditional direction is included#If and only if includes both directions.
~C. Only the inverse direction is included#If and only if is not the inverse.
~D. Both statement parts are negated#Negation is not what creates a biconditional.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which pair of statements is combined by p if and only if q?
{
~A. If p, then q and if not p, then not q#This pairs the conditional with the inverse.
~B. If not p, then not q and if not q, then not p#These are inverse and contrapositive forms.
=C. If p, then q and if q, then p#Correct. A biconditional combines the conditional and converse.
~D. If p, then not q and if q, then not p#These do not represent a biconditional.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
A student says the inverse of If p, then q is If q, then p. What is the mistake?
{
~A. The student wrote the contrapositive#The contrapositive is If not q, then not p.
=B. The student wrote the converse instead of the inverse#Correct. If q, then p switches the order without negating both parts.
~C. The student wrote the original conditional#The original conditional is If p, then q.
~D. The student wrote a biconditional#A biconditional uses if and only if.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
A student says the contrapositive of If p, then q is If not p, then not q. What is the mistake?
{
~A. The student wrote the converse#The converse is If q, then p.
~B. The student wrote the original conditional#The original conditional is If p, then q.
=C. The student wrote the inverse instead of the contrapositive#Correct. If not p, then not q is the inverse.
~D. The student wrote a biconditional#A biconditional uses if and only if.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
For the conditional If a polygon has four sides, then it is a quadrilateral, which statement is the converse?
{
=A. If a polygon is a quadrilateral, then it has four sides#Correct. The converse switches the hypothesis and conclusion.
~B. If a polygon does not have four sides, then it is not a quadrilateral#This is the inverse.
~C. If a polygon is not a quadrilateral, then it does not have four sides#This is the contrapositive.
~D. If a polygon has four sides, then it is not a quadrilateral#This negates only the conclusion.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
For the conditional If two lines are perpendicular, then they intersect, which statement is the inverse?
{
~A. If two lines intersect, then they are perpendicular#This is the converse.
~B. If two lines do not intersect, then they are not perpendicular#This is the contrapositive.
~C. If two lines are perpendicular, then they do not intersect#This negates only the conclusion.
=D. If two lines are not perpendicular, then they do not intersect#Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
For the conditional If an angle is acute, then its measure is less than 90°, which statement is the contrapositive?
{
~A. If an angle measure is less than 90°, then the angle is acute#This is the converse.
=B. If an angle measure is not less than 90°, then the angle is not acute#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~C. If an angle is not acute, then its measure is not less than 90°#This is the inverse.
~D. If an angle is acute, then its measure is not less than 90°#This negates only the conclusion.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which first step best supports this lesson skill?
{
~A. Guess from the picture before checking labels.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. Identify the givens and the geometric object before choosing a relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Conditional Statements, Converses, Inverses, and Contrapositives.
~C. Skip directly to the final answer.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. Use a formula that is not connected to the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which explanation best shows mastery of this lesson?
{
~A. The answer only states a number.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. The answer says the diagram looks correct.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. The answer copies vocabulary without using it.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=D. The answer names the evidence, applies the correct relationship, and interprets the conclusion.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Conditional Statements, Converses, Inverses, and Contrapositives.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What should a student check before submitting work for this lesson?
{
=A. That labels, notation, units, and reasoning match the lesson relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Conditional Statements, Converses, Inverses, and Contrapositives.
~B. That every answer is longer than the choices.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. That no diagram or evidence is included.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. That the work uses a future unit topic.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which error should be avoided in this lesson?
{
~A. Writing the given information first.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. Explaining why a theorem applies.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=C. Using appearance or an unrelated formula instead of the evidence in the task.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Conditional Statements, Converses, Inverses, and Contrapositives.
~D. Checking the final answer in context.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U01_L06_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L06_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which response is most aligned to the lesson objective?
{
~A. A response using a topic from a later unit.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. A step-by-step solution for Conditional Statements, Converses, Inverses, and Contrapositives that cites the correct evidence.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Conditional Statements, Converses, Inverses, and Contrapositives.
~C. A response with no labels or explanation.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. A response that changes the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
